+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: `iprange`
+
+Property-based tests were added with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest` 0.28.2)
+in `src/ipnet.rs`, module `hegel_tests` (17 tests, 12 distinct property families).
+
+## Properties tested
+
+All properties are grounded in the crate's documented contracts (rustdoc on
+`add`/`remove`/`merge`/`intersect`/`exclude`/`simplify`/`contains`/`supernet`)
+and its existing unit tests. Since the sibling crate `ipnet` yielded two
+top-of-address-space saturation bugs, the generators here explicitly boost
+`0.0.0.0/0`, `255.255.255.255`, `::/0`, `u32::MAX`/`u128::MAX`, and
+`1 << 31` / `1 << 127` boundaries.
+
+1. **Stateful model test** (`window_model_agrees`) — an `IpRange<Ipv4Net>` is
+   driven against an exact `BTreeSet<u8>` membership model over the 256-address
+   window `192.168.5.0/24`. Rules: `add`/`remove` of arbitrary window subnets
+   (prefix 24–32, so octets 0 and 255 are reachable), `remove` of a strict
+   supernet of the window (prefix 0–23, including `0.0.0.0/0` — must clear the
+   window), and `simplify` at arbitrary points. Invariant after every step:
+   `contains` agrees with the model at all 256 window addresses, `is_empty`
+   agrees, and expanding every iterated network reproduces the model exactly
+   (also proving no network ever escapes the window). Evidence: `add`/`remove`
+   docs (splitting semantics), `is_empty` doc example.
+2. **`merge` is membership-wise union** (IPv4 + IPv6 variants) — for arbitrary
+   network lists A, B: `merge(A,B).contains(x) == A.contains(x) || B.contains(x)`
+   for probes drawn at random plus every network's first/last address ±1 and
+   the space extremes 0 and MAX. Evidence: `merge` rustdoc ("in `self` or in
+   `other`").
+3. **`intersect` is membership-wise intersection** (IPv4) — same oracle with
+   `&&`. Evidence: `intersect` rustdoc.
+4. **`intersect` commutes** (IPv4) — `A ∩ B == B ∩ A` as `IpRange` equality
+   (the trie is canonical after simplification, and both operands are
+   simplified per the docs).
+5. **`exclude` is membership-wise set difference** (IPv4 + IPv6) —
+   `A.exclude(B).contains(x) == A.contains(x) && !B.contains(x)`. Evidence:
+   `exclude` rustdoc ("in `self` while not in `other`").
+6. **Universe partition round-trip / top-of-space saturation**
+   (`exclude_then_merge_restores_universe_v4`/`_v6`) — for arbitrary B:
+   `({0.0.0.0/0} \ B) ∪ B == {0.0.0.0/0}` (and `::/0` for IPv6). This is the
+   direct analogue of the ipnet trophy bugs: it forces full-range splits,
+   re-merges at every level up to the root, and equality against the one-leaf
+   universal trie.
+7. **`merge` self-identity** — `A ∪ A == A` (simplified `IpRange` equality).
+8. **`exclude` self-annihilation** — `A \ A` is empty (`is_empty()`).
+9. **`simplify` preserves membership** — an un-simplified range built by raw
+   `add`s answers `contains` identically before and after `simplify()`.
+   Evidence: `simplify` rustdoc ("combining networks", not changing the set).
+10. **`simplify` is idempotent** — simplifying twice equals simplifying once.
+11. **Iteration yields a disjoint, minimal, exact cover**
+    (`iter_yields_disjoint_minimal_exact_cover`) — for a collected
+    (simplified) range: every input network is covered; every yielded network
+    is contained and truncated; yielded networks are pairwise disjoint (using
+    `ipnet::Ipv4Net::contains` as an independent oracle); no yielded network's
+    sibling is also contained (minimality — otherwise `simplify` should have
+    merged them); and re-collecting the yielded networks reproduces the range
+    exactly (iterator/constructor round-trip).
+12. **`supernet` agrees with the `ipnet` containment oracle** — if
+    `supernet(q)` returns `Some(s)`, then `s.contains(q)` per `ipnet` and `s`
+    is one of the iterated member networks; if `None`, no member contains `q`.
+    Evidence: `supernet` rustdoc.
+13. **serde/bincode round-trip** (`serde_bincode_roundtrip_v4`/`_v6`, gated on
+    `--features serde`) — serialize/deserialize preserves `IpRange` equality
+    for arbitrary ranges, generalizing the existing hand-written
+    `serialize_ipv4_as_binary` unit tests.
+
+Generator notes: networks are generated over the full address space with all
+prefix lengths (0–32 / 0–128) and are deliberately **not** truncated — the
+implementation only reads the prefix bits, so host bits must not matter, and
+the tests verify that implicitly.
+
+## Test results
+
+- `cargo test` (default features): **67 passed, 0 failed** (15 hegel tests +
+  52 pre-existing unit tests) plus 6 doctests.
+- `cargo test --features serde`: **71 passed, 0 failed** (17 hegel tests).
+- Liveness check: the union oracle in `merge_matches_membership_union` was
+  temporarily inverted (`||` → `&&`); hegel failed it and shrank to the
+  minimal counterexample `a = [], b = [0.0.0.0/0]`, probe `0.0.0.0`. Reverted.
+- Exploratory high-count run: all 17 hegel tests run at
+  `test_cases = 1000` (10x the committed default), with the serde feature
+  enabled — all passed (14s). Committed tests use the default 100 cases.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+None found. In particular, the boundaries that broke the sibling `ipnet`
+crate (trophies 22–23) hold here: full-range `0.0.0.0/0` / `::/0` handling,
+`255.255.255.255/32` and `ff...ff/128` membership, top-of-space splits and
+re-merges to a saturated universe trie, and `u32::MAX`/`u128::MAX` probes all
+behave correctly, at both the default and 10x case counts. The radix-trie
+representation avoids the end-of-range arithmetic (`broadcast + 1`) where the
+ipnet bugs lived, which is consistent with this outcome.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **BFS iteration order is sorted by address** — not asserted: the iterator
+  is documented as BFS over the trie, which orders by prefix length first
+  (the existing `iter_ipv4` unit test sorts before comparing, confirming
+  order is not part of the contract).
+- **`contains(network)` equals "every address of the network is in the set"
+  for *un-simplified* ranges** — `contains` requires a single covering member
+  network in the trie; before `simplify()`, a network fully covered by two
+  adjacent leaves is reported as not contained. This is documented behavior
+  (`add` explicitly says it does not combine networks), so membership-set
+  properties are asserted only on simplified ranges (collected ranges and the
+  results of `merge`/`intersect`/`exclude` are documented as simplified; the
+  state-machine model checks single addresses, for which this distinction
+  does not arise).
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Rust reference was accurate throughout: `#[hegel::composite]`,
+  `one_of!` + `sampled_from` boundary boosting, plain `&TestCase` helper
+  functions, and `#[hegel::state_machine]` all worked first try on this
+  edition-2024 crate.
+- The tip that there is no global case-count override (gotcha 13) was needed
+  for the 10x exploratory run; a `HEGEL_TEST_CASES`-style env var would make
+  that step less invasive than editing 17 attributes and reverting.
+- The state-machine section's note that invariants run on the initial state
+  mattered here (the empty-range/empty-model invariant must hold before any
+  rule).
+- `IpRange`'s `Debug` impl truncates to 4 networks, so shrunk counterexample
+  output for drawn `Vec<Ipv4Net>` values (shown fully) was more informative
+  than the range itself — the skill's advice to draw plain data and construct
+  the subject in the test worked well.
